b70-cuda: make hipEventRecord / cudaEventRecord cheap on the Level Zero backend

Measured on an Arc Pro B70 (Xe2), compute-runtime 26.05:
  cudaEventRecord, idle GPU      480 us -> 9 us
  kernel + record per iteration  292 us -> 28 us (kernel alone: 22 us)

1. The recorded device timestamp is copied by the GPU into the event object.
   Into ordinary pageable host memory that 8-byte copy costs ~300 us; into USM
   host memory ~10 us. Timestamps now live in slots carved from USM host slabs.
2. Each re-record of a user event created a fresh single-slot event pool
   (issue #1258 workaround), ~300 us per zeEventPoolCreate. Retired slots that
   have already signaled are exactly as unreferenced as the ones the reaper
   destroys, so up to 8 are kept as spares and recycled with a host reset.
3. The host timestamp only orders two events in getElapsedTime(); use a host
   monotonic clock instead of a zeDeviceGetGlobalTimestamps call (~15 us).

Applies to chipStar 092b55c886dcd25145e0ba5882693ef03b76140a.

